# Git performs no end-of-line conversion, on any platform, in either direction.
#
# gitattributes(5), on unsetting the text attribute:
#   "Unsetting the text attribute on a path tells Git not to attempt any
#    end-of-line conversion upon checkin or checkout."
#
# This lives here rather than in git config because attributes travel with the
# repository and configuration does not. Git itself converts nothing by default:
# core.autocrlf is unset out of the box. The Git for Windows *installer* is what
# turns conversion on, and its preselected option does exactly that:
#
#   case ReplayChoice('CRLF Option','CRLFAlways') of
#     -- installer/install.iss line 2377, where the second argument is the default
#
#   'true':  RecordInferredDefault('CRLF Option','CRLFAlways');
#   'false': RecordInferredDefault('CRLF Option','CRLFCommitAsIs');
#   'input': RecordInferredDefault('CRLF Option','LFOnly');
#     -- install.iss lines 870-872, mapping each option to core.autocrlf
#
#   https://github.com/git-for-windows/build-extra/blob/7b61c1c82052183d0d865fa807d69636c5245dbc/installer/install.iss#L2377
#
# So a clone on a stock Windows install gets core.autocrlf=true, which
# git-config(1) describes as "the same as setting the text attribute to 'auto'
# on all files and core.eol to 'crlf'", and therefore a CRLF working tree. The
# line below makes that impossible regardless of how any contributor configured
# their Git.
#
# What the bytes actually are is EditorConfig's job (.editorconfig), not Git's.

* -text
